Rules to make a class immutable
Usually you follow these rules:
1. Make the class final
2. Make all fields private
3. Make instance fields final
4. Initialize all fields through the constructor
5. Do not provide setters
6. If a field refers to a mutable object, use defensive copying
7. Do not expose mutable internal objects directly from getters


examample 
public final class Employee {

    private final int id;
    private final String name;

    public Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}


What exactly does final mean?
This is extremely important.
For primitives:
private final int age;

once initialized:
age = 30;

you cannot later do:
age = 40;

So for primitives, final effectively fixes the value.
But with objects, final behaves differently.
Consider:
private final List<String> names;

final means:
The reference cannot point to another list.

It does not mean:
The List itself cannot change.

Example:
final List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add("A");
list.add("B");

This is legal.
But this is not:
list = new ArrayList<>();


Problem 1: constructor reference escape
Imagine:
List<String> subjects = new ArrayList<>();

subjects.add("Math");
subjects.add("Physics");

Student student = new Student("Shubham", subjects);

Inside student:
student.subjects
       |
       v
+----------------+
| Math           |
| Physics        |
+----------------+
       ^
       |
local subjects

Both references point to exactly the same ArrayList.
Now outside the class:
subjects.add("Chemistry");

What happens to the student?
Its internal list also appears to change:
System.out.println(student.getSubjects());

Output:
[Math, Physics, Chemistry]




Solution: defensive copy in the constructor
Instead of:
this.subjects = subjects;

do:
this.subjects = new ArrayList<>(subjects);

Now:
public Student(String name, List<String> subjects) {
    this.name = name;
    this.subjects = new ArrayList<>(subjects);
}

Then the caller has:
Caller list
+----------------+
| Math           |
| Physics        |
+----------------+

and your Student has its own copy:
Student list
+----------------+
| Math           |
| Physics        |
+----------------+

If caller does:
subjects.add("Chemistry");

only the caller's list changes.
Your Student remains unchanged.
That's called a defensive copy.
But there is still another problem
Even after constructor copying:
public final class Student {

    private final String name;
    private final List<String> subjects;

    public Student(String name, List<String> subjects) {
        this.name = name;
        this.subjects = new ArrayList<>(subjects);
    }

    public List<String> getSubjects() {
        return subjects;
    }
}

someone can do:
student.getSubjects().add("Chemistry");

Why?
Because getSubjects() returns the actual internal list.
You have leaked your mutable state.
So again:
Outside code
     |
     v
getSubjects()
     |
     v
Student's actual internal List

The outside code can modify it.
Solution: defensive copy in getter
Instead of:
public List<String> getSubjects() {
    return subjects;
}

use:
public List<String> getSubjects() {
    return new ArrayList<>(subjects);
}

Now callers get a copy.




Modern Java approach: List.copyOf()
A cleaner approach is often:
public final class Student {

    private final String name;
    private final List<String> subjects;

    public Student(String name, List<String> subjects) {
        this.name = name;
        this.subjects = List.copyOf(subjects);
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public List<String> getSubjects() {
        return subjects;
    }
}

List.copyOf() creates an unmodifiable list representation.
Now:
student.getSubjects().add("Chemistry");

throws:
UnsupportedOperationException
